---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import LinkCards from "../../components/docs/LinkCards.astro";
import { docHref } from "../../data/docs";

const sections = [
  { id: "what-a-team-is", title: "What a team is" },
  { id: "turn-on", title: "Turn on team execution" },
  { id: "create", title: "Create a team" },
  { id: "start", title: "Start a team conversation" },
  { id: "talk", title: "Talk to the team" },
  { id: "members", title: "How members work together" },
  { id: "changes", title: "Review and combine changes" },
  { id: "needs-you", title: "When the team needs you" },
  { id: "limits", title: "Limits" },
  { id: "next-steps", title: "Next steps" },
];
---

<DocsLayout slug="teams" sections={sections}>
  <p>You talk to a team the way you talk to a single agent. The lead answers, other members join in when you address them, and the lead can hand pieces of the work to the members who report to it.</p>

  <section aria-labelledby="what-a-team-is">
    <h2 id="what-a-team-is">What a team is</h2>
    <p>You save teams per project. A team has up to 12 members:</p>
    <dl class="docs-terms">
      <div>
        <dt>Lead</dt>
        <dd>The member who replies when you don't address anyone else. It plans, answers, and hands out work.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Members</dt>
        <dd>Each one has a name, a responsibility in your own words, and its own model and effort. A seat can also go to one of your <a href={docHref("orclings")}>Orclings</a>.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Hierarchy</dt>
        <dd>Every member reports to the lead or to another member, at most three levels deep: lead, managers and workers.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>There are no fixed roles. The lead reads each member's responsibility to decide what to hand it. Every save makes a new version of the team, and a team conversation keeps the version it started with.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="turn-on">
    <h2 id="turn-on">Turn on team execution</h2>
    <p><strong>Team execution (Beta)</strong> is on by default. It's in <a href={docHref("settings") + "#general"}>Settings</a> under <strong>General</strong>, in the <strong>Orchestration</strong> group. With it off, you can still design and save teams, but no team work can start. A team that's already working keeps going, and <strong>Stop</strong> stays available.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="create">
    <h2 id="create">Create a team</h2>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Open <strong>Orchestration</strong> in the sidebar. If you've hidden it, it's under <strong>More</strong>.</li>
      <li>Choose the project. If it already has a team, click <strong>New team</strong>, the plus button beside the team list.</li>
      <li>Enter a <strong>Team name</strong>.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Add member</strong> once for each agent. The first member is the lead, and <strong>Make lead</strong> moves the role to someone else.</li>
      <li>For each member, set the <strong>Member name</strong>, who it <strong>Reports to</strong>, its <strong>Model and effort</strong>, and its <strong>Responsibility</strong>.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Check readiness</strong>. OpenOrc checks that each member's agent is installed and signed in, and that its model is available.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Save team</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The editor also holds the team's <strong>Discussion</strong> settings and, under <strong>Execution limits</strong>, how far each run may go.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="start">
    <h2 id="start">Start a team conversation</h2>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Click <strong>New thread</strong> and choose the project. Teams aren't available in the Workspace.</li>
      <li>Open the model picker and switch to <strong>Project teams</strong>. Each team shows its size and version, such as <strong>4 agents · Version 2</strong>. Pick one.</li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Local checkout</strong> or <strong>Worktree</strong> for where the team works.</li>
      <li>Write your first message and send it.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The project needs Git and at least one commit. If no team can start, the picker shows what to fix, such as <strong>Open Settings</strong> or <strong>Create a team in Orchestration</strong>.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="talk">
    <h2 id="talk">Talk to the team</h2>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">You write</th>
            <th scope="col">Who gets the message</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>No mention</td>
            <td>The lead.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>@Name</code></td>
            <td>That member. Type <code>@</code> to pick from the team.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>@everyone</code></td>
            <td>The whole team. <code>@all</code> works too.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>When the team has finished, your next message starts it again. While it's working, <kbd>Enter</kbd> sends your message into the turns already running, where the agent can take it. <strong>Queue</strong> holds it for the next turn instead. Under your message, each recipient's delivery reads <strong>Sent</strong>, <strong>Sending…</strong> or <strong>Queued for next turn</strong>, and <strong>Seen by</strong> lists who has read it. A message waiting for the lead offers <strong>Send now</strong> and <strong>Cancel queued message</strong>.</p>
    <p>The mode menu applies to the whole team. Agents already working switch after they finish their turn. From the composer you can change the lead's effort and Fast mode, but not its model. An Orcling lead keeps its own settings.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="members">
    <h2 id="members">How members work together</h2>
    <p>Every member takes part in the conversation and shares the team's folder. A member you address replies in the chat, where you see it. Members address each other the same way, and anything said in the chat reaches the rest as context on their next turn.</p>
    <p>Members are told to claim files before they edit them in the shared folder. A member's turn then shows <strong>Holding:</strong> with the files. A colleague can't claim those files until they're released, but nothing locks the files themselves.</p>
    <p>For larger, bounded work, the lead or a manager hands an assignment to a member who reports to it. An assignment runs in a worktree of its own, built from its manager's current files. Its card shows the member, <strong>Delegated by</strong> and the manager's name, and a state such as <strong>Working</strong> or <strong>Waiting for reports</strong>. <strong>Open assignment</strong> opens it as a task.</p>
    <p>By default, members work only when addressed. In the team's <strong>Discussion</strong> settings, <strong>Reads per message</strong> lets members read new messages on their own and chime in, and <strong>Follow-ups after colleagues</strong> lets them answer each other. Each read is an extra model turn, so the default is <strong>Off (recommended)</strong>.</p>
    <p>To see each member's steps, open the team menu in the toolbar, which shows the team's status, and turn on <strong>Show activity</strong>.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="changes">
    <h2 id="changes">Review and combine changes</h2>
    <p>A turn that changes files in the shared folder ends with a change card and a <strong>Review</strong> button. Its note, <strong>Shared workspace checkpoint · may include concurrent edits</strong>, means other members may have edited the folder at the same time. Team change cards have no <strong>Undo</strong>.</p>
    <p>Assignments come back on their own. When one finishes, OpenOrc merges its files into its manager's folder before the manager's next turn, and the manager reports the combined result up the hierarchy. If the merge conflicts, a <strong>Changes need review</strong> card lists the conflicting files:</p>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Click <strong>Open retained integration</strong> to open the folder where the merge stopped.</li>
      <li>Resolve the conflicting files there.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Accept resolved changes</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>Retry integration</strong> merges the current folders again in a new folder instead. When the team is done, review and commit its changes as you would for any thread. See <a href={docHref("review")}>Changes and review</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="needs-you">
    <h2 id="needs-you">When the team needs you</h2>
    <p>Approvals and questions from any member collect in a <strong>Needs you</strong> panel above the composer. When several are waiting, a list shows how many are pending. <strong>View context</strong> scrolls to the request in the conversation.</p>
    <p>A member that fails shows what went wrong, with buttons to recover:</p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Button</th>
            <th scope="col">What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Retry assignment</strong></td>
            <td>Runs the turn again. Each retry counts toward <strong>Attempts per assignment</strong>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Start fresh session</strong></td>
            <td>Starts the agent in a new session from a short handoff. History and files are kept.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Open workspace</strong></td>
            <td>Opens the member's folder so you can look at its files.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>If OpenOrc quits while a member is mid-turn, that member needs your attention when OpenOrc starts again, since OpenOrc can't tell what the turn did. Retrying that turn doesn't count toward its attempts. Turns that hadn't started yet run on their own.</p>
    <p><strong>Stop</strong> in the composer stops the team. OpenOrc notifies you when the team finishes, when a member needs attention, and when one waits for an approval or an answer.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="limits">
    <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
    <p>Each time the team runs, from your message until every member is done, it works within the team's <strong>Execution limits</strong>:</p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Limit</th>
            <th scope="col">Default</th>
            <th scope="col">Range</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Concurrent agents</strong></td>
            <td>3</td>
            <td>1 to 12</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Assignments per execution</strong></td>
            <td>24</td>
            <td>1 to 100</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Execution time (minutes)</strong></td>
            <td>60</td>
            <td>1 to 480</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Attempts per assignment</strong></td>
            <td>2</td>
            <td>1 to 5</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>When the time runs out, OpenOrc stops the team. Each run also has fixed caps: 1,000 agent turns, 2,000 messages, and 2 MiB of message text. Past a cap, OpenOrc refuses new turns or messages.</p>
    <Callout title="Beta limits">
      Every member's turn runs on your account with that member's agent, so a team uses your plan limits faster than a single agent does. Teams don't work with repositories that contain submodules or special files, such as sockets.
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next-steps">
    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <LinkCards cards={[{ slug: "messaging" }, { slug: "permissions" }, { slug: "review" }, { slug: "orclings" }]} />
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
